  "slug": "specific-topic-8dfd",
  "summary": "Five preprints link lysosomal state to microglial reprogramming and axonal quality control, with TRA2B lowering tau seeding thresholds and ankyrin-G tools for the AIS.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is lysosomal state as a determinant of neurodegenerative cell phenotypes. Two preprints tie lysosomal programs to disease-associated microglia (DAM): MiT/TFE-driven lysosomal biogenesis precedes and couples to DAM reprogramming across murine injury, amyloidosis and human AD, while GPNMB marks a reversible, TREM2-dependent lysosomal program that tracks neurodegeneration severity in FTD/AD models. In neurons, the axon initial segment is described as a lysosome quality-control checkpoint, with MCF2L anchoring lysosomes via gAnkG and retaining less acidic ones, and a separate preprint engineers a high-affinity ankyrin-G binder for targeting the AIS. A further preprint identifies TRA2B as a latent endogenous modifier that lowers the tau seeding threshold. New here is the focus on lysosomal programs as measurable, compartment-specific readouts that scale with pathology.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
